# Documentation and UI language policy

Edbert is multilingual from the start: **English (authoritative) and German**.

## Technical documents — English only

Specification (`docs/spec/`), backlog, rules, skills, code, comments, docstrings, commit messages, issues, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `catalog/README.md`, example descriptions. Never translate them.

## User-facing documents — English + German

| What | English (authoritative) | German (translation) |
|---|---|---|
| Landing page | `README.md` | `README.de.md` |
| User manuals | `docs/user/en/*.md` | `docs/user/de/*.md` (same file names) |

- **Edit English first.** The German page follows. English wins if they differ.
- Every German page starts with a short block naming the English source, e.g. `<!-- source: docs/user/en/<file>.md @ <commit> -->`. When the English page changes, the German page must be updated or marked outdated.
- Keep code identifiers, JSON keys, file paths, env vars and CLI commands **verbatim** in both languages.
- A new user page is created in both languages in the same change, or the German one is listed as an open item in `backlog/Backlog-Bugfixes.md` under `## Document Review Findings`.
- `tests/test_docs_translation.py` checks that every English user page has a German counterpart.

## UI texts

- All visible strings go through the translation catalogs `src/edbert/i18n/catalogs/{en,de}.json` via `Translator.t(key, **params)`. **Never hard-code visible text.**
- Every key must exist in all languages with the same placeholders (checked by `tests/test_i18n_catalogs.py`).
- Error messages from the engine are `EdbertError` with a stable `code` and parameters; the UI translates the code (`error.<CODE>` keys). The English message stays for logs.
- Do not keep language state in module globals (Streamlit serves several sessions from one process): use one `Translator` per session.
- Widget keys and stored values are language independent; show translated text only as the label (`format_func`).
- Names of suppliers and products are proper nouns and are not translated.

## Legal texts — per country, in the national language

- Terms, privacy notices and withdrawal information are written **per country in the national language**: Germany → German, Austria → German, Switzerland → German. Each country has its own version (different law).
- They are **drafts outside this repository** (`Earnie-Projekt/Entwicklungsplan/Offen/Edbert-Bedingungstexte/`) until legally reviewed; do not add or paraphrase them here.
- The English-authoritative principle applies to technical and user documents, **not** to legal texts. Which version binds consumers is a question for the legal review.

## Relation to other rules

- `english-chat.mdc`: assistant chat language in Cursor. In Claude Code the language follows `CLAUDE.md`.
- `edbert-project.mdc`: license and legal-text guardrails.
